<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinese philosophy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chinese philosophy is the body of philosophical traditions that arose and developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It began in earnest in the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>, the age of the Hundred Schools of Thought, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laozi">Laozi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>, Mozi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xunzi-philosopher">Xunzi</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, Daoism, Mohism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> Confucianism became the official ideology, and Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> later fed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Ming dynasties. Buddhist and Neo-Confucian thought spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and modern thinkers answered Western thought with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-principles-of-the-people">Three Principles of the People</a>, Chinese Marxism and New Confucianism.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…great eastern state of ancient China for eight centuries: under Duke Huan and Guan Zhong it created the first hegemony over the Zhou states, its salt, iron and textile wealth made Linzi one of the largest cities of its age, and its Jixia Academy shaped classical <b>Chinese philosophy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
